// Practice quiz: Pagination and sorting
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-data-jpa/pagination-and-sorting

::o1 parameters::Which query parameters fill a Pageable by default? {
~offset and limit#Not quite. Those are not the default names.
~start and count#Not quite. Those are not the default names.
~from and to#Not quite. Those are not the default names.
=page, size and sort#Correct. Spring Data web support reads these.
}

::o2 first page::Which page number is the first page by default? {
=0#Correct. Spring Data pages are zero-based.
~1#Not quite. One-based pages need a setting.
~-1#Not quite. Negative pages are invalid.
~It depends on the database#Not quite. This is Spring Data behavior.
}

::o2 total pages::For 25 rows and size 10, how many pages are there? {
~2#Not quite. That loses the last 5 rows.
=3#Correct. 25 divided by 10, rounded up.
~10#Not quite. That is the size.
~25#Not quite. That is the number of rows.
}

::o2 count query::Why might a Page run two SQL statements? {
~Hibernate runs everything twice#Not quite. It does not.
=One fetches the rows and one counts the total#Correct. Page metadata needs the total.
~One statement is for logging#Not quite. Logging runs no SQL.
~Sorting needs its own query#Not quite. Sorting is in the same statement.
}

::o3 stable shape::What gives a stable JSON shape for paged responses? {
~Returning PageImpl directly#Not quite. Its JSON has no stability guarantee.
~Using a larger page size#Not quite. Size does not affect the shape.
=Returning PagedModel or using via-dto serialization#Correct. They provide a defined content and page structure.
~Removing the sort#Not quite. Sort does not affect the shape.
}

